# BizMastas

Real-time monitoring dashboard for the **impregnation line** at Biashara Masters Sawmill
(MDF, HDF, chipboard and lamination). It reads the line PLC over Modbus TCP and shows oven
temperatures, drives, safety inputs and alarms in a Streamlit web app.

## Features

- Six-oven overview: actual vs setpoint, with OK / WARNING / CRITICAL status
- Drives and process: main drive, line speed, hydraulic pump and pressure, unwinder, rewinder
- Safety inputs: E-stop, resin level, board sensor
- Alarm table (critical first) and a live banner
- Oven temperature trend chart (last 200 samples)
- Auto-refresh with a configurable interval, plus a manual refresh button
- Shift label (Day / Afternoon / Night, Africa/Nairobi time), operator notes, CSV shift report
- Simulation mode when the PLC is unreachable, clearly marked on screen

## Quick start

```bash
git clone https://github.com/thee-collo20/BizMastas.git
cd BizMastas
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate        # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
streamlit run app.py
```

No PLC? The app falls back to simulated data automatically, or tick **Force simulation mode**
in the sidebar for demos.

## Configuration

Everything lives in `config.py`.

| Setting | Purpose | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `PLC_CONFIG` | PLC IP, port, timeout | `192.168.1.88`, `502`, 2 s |
| `REGISTER_MAP` | Where each value lives in the PLC | see below |
| `THRESHOLDS` | Alarm limits | warn 2 °C, crit 5 °C, speed tolerance 0.5 m/min |
| `TIMEZONE` | Timestamps and shift labels | `Africa/Nairobi` |
| `REFRESH_SECONDS` | Default auto-refresh | 5 |

The PLC address and port can also be set without editing code:

```bash
export BIZMASTAS_PLC_IP=192.168.1.50
export BIZMASTAS_PLC_PORT=502
```

### Register map

| Table | Address | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Holding | 0-5 | Oven 1-6 actual temperature |
| Holding | 10-15 | Oven 1-6 setpoint |
| Holding | 20 / 21 | Line speed actual / setpoint |
| Holding | 22 | Hydraulic pressure |
| Coils | 0 / 1 / 2 / 3 | Main drive / hydraulic pump / unwinder / rewinder running |
| Discrete inputs | 0 / 1 | E-stop OK / resin low |
| Input registers | 0 / 1 | Board present / line temperature |

Check these against the PLC program before relying on the dashboard. Modbus registers are
16-bit integers, so if the PLC stores temperatures scaled (for example 1450 for 145.0 °C),
divide the value in the `logic/` module that reads it.

### Alarm rules

| Alarm | Level | Condition |
|---|---|---|
| Oven drifting | WARN | \|actual - setpoint\| > 2 °C |
| Oven off target | CRIT | \|actual - setpoint\| > 5 °C |
| Main drive stopped | CRIT | main drive coil off |
| Line speed unstable | WARN | drive running and speed differs from setpoint by 0.5 m/min or more |
| E-stop pressed | CRIT | E-stop input not OK |
| Resin level low | WARN | resin-low input on |
| PLC read failed | WARN | a Modbus table could not be read |

## Project layout

```
app.py             Streamlit dashboard (UI, auto-refresh, report)
config.py          PLC settings, register map, thresholds
modbus_client.py   Modbus TCP reads (readAll, FactoryPLC) and the simulator
logic/
  ovens.py         Six-oven temperature logic
  motors.py        Drives, pump, line speed
  sensors.py       Safety and process inputs
  alarms.py        Combines everything into one alarm list
tests/             pytest tests for the logic modules
```

`app.py` calls each module in `logic/` and combines the results, so a new part of the line
means a new module plus a few lines in `app.py`.

## Tests

```bash
pip install pytest
python -m pytest tests
```

## Notes

- The dashboard is read-only: it never writes to the PLC.
- It is a monitoring aid, not a safety system. Do not use it in place of the machine's own
  E-stop and interlocks.

